**Bubble Sort implementation (JavaScript)**  

```js
/**
 * Bubble Sort – sorts an array of integers in ascending order.
 *
 * Algorithm overview:
 *   Repeatedly step through the list, compare adjacent elements and swap
 *   them if they are in the wrong order. After each outer pass the largest
 *   unsorted element “bubbles” to its final position at the end of the array.
 *   The process stops early when a full pass makes no swaps (the array is
 *   already sorted).
 *
 * Complexity:
 *   • Time   – O(n²) in the worst and average case,
 *              O(n) best case when the input is already sorted (thanks to the
 *              early‑exit optimisation).
 *   • Space  – O(1) auxiliary space (the sort is performed in‑place).
 *
 * @param {number[]} input   The array to be sorted.
 * @returns {number[]}       A **new** sorted array (the original array is left
 *                           untouched).
 *
 * @throws {TypeError}  If the argument is not an array.
 * @throws {Error}      If the array length exceeds 100 or contains a value
 *                      outside the allowed range [-10 000, 10 000].
 */
function bubbleSort(input) {
  // ---------- 1️⃣  Input validation ----------
  if (!Array.isArray(input)) {
    throw new TypeError('bubbleSort: input must be an array');
  }

  const MAX_LENGTH = 100;
  const MIN_VALUE  = -10_000;
  const MAX_VALUE  = 10_000;

  if (input.length > MAX_LENGTH) {
    throw new Error(`bubbleSort: array length (${input.length}) exceeds the maximum allowed ${MAX_LENGTH}`);
  }

  // Verify each element is an integer within the specified range.
  for (let i = 0; i < input.length; i++) {
    const val = input[i];
    if (!Number.isInteger(val)) {
      throw new TypeError(`bubbleSort: element at index ${i} is not an integer (found ${val})`);
    }
    if (val < MIN_VALUE || val > MAX_VALUE) {
      throw new Error(`bubbleSort: element at index ${i} (${val}) is outside the allowed range [${MIN_VALUE}, ${MAX_VALUE}]`);
    }
  }

  // ---------- 2️⃣  Make a shallow copy so we don't mutate the caller ----------
  const arr = input.slice();   // work on a copy to keep the original array intact

  const n = arr.length;
  // Flag to detect when the array becomes sorted early
  let swapped;

  // ---------- 3️⃣  Core Bubble Sort loops ----------
  for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    swapped = false;   // assume no swaps this pass

    // After each outer iteration, the last i elements are already sorted
    for (let j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
      // Compare adjacent items
      if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
        // Swap if they are out of order
        const temp = arr[j];
        arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
        arr[j + 1] = temp;
        swapped = true;   // a swap happened, so we need another pass
      }
    }

    // If no swaps occurred, the array is completely sorted → exit early
    if (!swapped) break;
  }

  // ---------- 4️⃣  Return the sorted copy ----------
  return arr;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Example usage (you can delete or comment this out in production)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
try {
  const rawData = [64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90];
  const sorted = bubbleSort(rawData);
  console.log('Original:', rawData);
  console.log('Sorted  :', sorted);
} catch (e) {
  console.error('Error:', e.message);
}
```

### How to use it
```js
const validArray = [3, -5, 17, 0, 9];
const result = bubbleSort(validArray); // → [-5, 0, 3, 9, 17]

// Invalid usage examples (will throw)
try {
  bubbleSort([1, 2, 150]);               // → Error: element out of range
  bubbleSort('not an array');            // → TypeError
  bubbleSort(new Array(120).fill(0));    // → Error: array length exceeds limit
} catch (err) {
  console.error(err.message);
}
```

The function validates the input, sorts the copy in‑place, and returns a new sorted array while preserving the original data. All edge‑cases (non‑array input, length limits, non‑integer values, out‑of‑range numbers) are handled with clear error messages. Happy coding!

