## 5️⃣ Design Rationale & Quick Customisation Tips

| Design Element | Why it Works | How to Tweak |
|----------------|--------------|--------------|
| **Color Palette** | Earthy green + copper evokes ancient stone and metal artifacts while staying modern. | Change `--primary` & `--accent` values in CSS to match your brand or excavation colors (e.g., gray for a museum vibe). |
| **Rounded Cards & Shadows** | Makes each artifact feel like a tangible object you can “lift”. | Adjust `border-radius` or `box-shadow` values for sharper or softer effects. |
| **Hero Section with Full‑Bleed Image** | Instantly tells visitors the site’s purpose; visually impressive. | Replace the background URL with a site‑specific image (e.g., a dramatic excavation shot). |
| **Grid Layout for Teasers** | Flexible – automatically adapts to screen width. | Add more `<a class="artifact-card">…</a>` blocks; modify `minmax(250px,1fr)` if you want larger thumbnails on larger screens. |
| **Search Box with Border Highlight on Focus** | Provides an obvious, approachable entry point; smooth interaction. | Add placeholder icons or suggestions using a small JS library if desired. |
| **Artifact Detail Page Layout** | Image on the left, text right → reading flow mimics a museum label beside a specimen. | Swap with column order in CSS (`flex-direction: row-reverse;`) for mobile‑first design, or increase `flex-basis` for larger images. |
| **Context Box** | Sets the artifact apart, emphasises provenance, and adds depth. | Use `border-left:4px solid #FF6B6B` for a pop of red if you want to highlight a particular section. |
| **Responsive Adjustments** | Guarantees readability on phones/tabs. | Add breakpoint media queries for typography scaling (`rem` sizes) or hide/expand certain elements. |

